    "taxonId": 131135,
    "summary": "An underwater seagrass found in the Mediterranean, along parts of the North African Atlantic coast, and around the Canary Islands and Madeira. It forms seabed meadows and has very narrow light-green or grey-green leaves; inconspicuous grass-like flowers sometimes appear in spring.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p3", "s1:p4"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 502780,
    "summary": "A shrub or small-to-medium tree native to southern Africa, from southern Angola east to Mozambique and south to South Africa. It is readily recognised by its exceptionally long paired white thorns and coffee-coloured bark.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Gum from cracks in the bark was used to make sweets and formerly had economic importance as Cape Gum.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p7"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p12"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 141535,
    "summary": "A thyme-moss originating in North America and now also found in Middle America, Europe, Africa and much of Asia. Its sharply toothed, gradually tapering leaves form green cushions; when dry, the leaves darken and curl.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p5"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 60287,
    "summary": "A perennial grass occurring wild mainly in northern North America and adjacent northeastern Siberia. Its dense, nodding greenish or purplish seed heads bear barbed bristles that readily attach to animals and can seriously injure grazing livestock when dry.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Kawaiisu people pounded and ate the seeds dry.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p5", "s1:p6"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p9"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 163932,
    "summary": "A small, fragile perennial fern most frequent in western Europe’s oceanic regions, especially the British Isles. Its translucent leaves are only a single cell thick, and creeping underground stems produce dense colonies of overlapping leaves.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p6"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 209505,
    "summary": "A scrambling shrubby vine native to central and western China. It is practically thornless, though stout shoots may carry a few prickles. Its small, fragrant white or pale-yellow flowers are among the earliest roses, usually opening in May in the Northern Hemisphere but later after cold weather.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p4", "s1:p5"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 59016,
    "summary": "A semiaquatic annual flowering herb native to much of the temperate world. It forms low fleshy tufts in mud or wet sand, with spoon-shaped leaves and tiny white flowers sometimes tinted pink or blue.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 59029,
    "summary": "A hairy biennial herb native to Europe, northern Africa and Asia. It forms a large leaf rosette followed by a tall stem densely packed with small yellow flowers. Its abundant seeds may remain able to germinate for decades.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p3", "s1:p35"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
